        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The outcome of the vote has significant political and societal consequences worth discussing.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes the issue is urgent and merits public attention.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener shares a common understanding of what constitutes 'violence' and 'the capital' in the context of January 6th.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener values informed discussion on high-stakes political issues.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 44476,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44476.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Look, what happened on January 6 needs to be investigated, understand what led to that, understand the aftermath of it, how, you know, rhetoric had contributed to this insurrection. And then why was the appropriate forces not prepared for something like this to happen? It wasn't like it was a secret that a lot of people were going to be amassing on January 6. And how come the tactics, techniques, and procedures of those involved have fell down? One of the reasons that I think members of Congress should be focused on this is for their staff. You had young men and women who from their town thought they were coming to Washington DC to help their hometown congressperson And there, a lot of them were scared and worried and having to hide in their office and not know what was potentially going to happen. It's also, for me, that is what happened on January 6 is an indication of this truth decay that we're seeing happening across the country. And when I joined the CIA back in October 2000, when I was in the CIA, when 9-11 happened, and if Islamic extremism was the existential crisis of the day in those early 2000s. I think this perpetuation and this true decay that's happening across and being exacerbated by social media is an existential crisis that we have right now. And I think a bipartisan, equal Republicans and Democrats of thoughtful people to investigate this and hopefully take some of the politics out of this is going to be important.",
