    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "The short answer, James, and thanks for that question, because it's a great question, something really I've been talking about for years, the short answer is yes. the threat of, there are still two bipartisan issues in Washington DC, and that's the threat of the Chinese government. And it's the need to, you know, to defend ourselves against cyber threats. And I want to make it clear, I always talk about the Chinese government. The beef is not with the Chinese people. The beef is obviously not with Chinese Americans. The amount of hate that are Chinese, Asian American brothers and sisters in this country are facing the hate crimes. It's just unacceptable. But we should be clear. It's the Chinese government. They are a peer. Their economy is going to be larger than the United States economy in a couple of years. They have, you know, they, I think this year is when the amount of research they were putting into advanced technologies or past our research, I would say that that number of pure dollar equivalent is not indicative. If you look at the man hours and woman hours of research, the Chinese probably surpassed us years ago. And so we have some serious challenges and the Chinese government has made it very clear. They're trying to surpass the United States of America as a leader in this country and they're going to do it by being the global leader in advanced technologies like 5G, AI, quantum computing space, all these things are interconnected. And if they have a larger economy, if they are able to drive the development of some of these tools, it's going to be based on their values. And they've already showed us what they value. They showed how they're using facial recognition and technology to basically put their ethnic minorities in the Uyghurs in internment camps and commit genocide. We know that they're going to try to export that kind of technology to other authoritarian regimes. And so the United States and our allies need to be driving the conversations on these technologies and how we use them in an ethical way. You know, I would say we got to take advantage of technology before it takes advantage of us. And we have to get the ethics and the proper use of these tools because it is coming. The destruction is going to be here. And we got to be ready for that. So that means we have to educate our kids and being able to operate in an environment and pursue jobs that don't exist today. We have to help current workers be able to transition to this new world. We know AI artificial intelligence is going to be able to produce code. So this isn't only going to impact, you know, a lot of times people talk about AI impacting blue collar jobs. It's going to impact white collar jobs as well too. And we can be ready and we can take advantage of this. And this technology is going to be able to do things like help us produce more crops with less water using less land. So we're going to be able to feed more people. we're going to be able to detect diseases before to get people that live healthier and safer lives. And so these tools are going to be great things that we need to be able to harness, but we have to do it the right way. And we are in a new Cold War with the Chinese government, period in its story. This new Cold War is going to be different. The Russians, the last time we were in the Cold War, The USSR was nowhere near the size of what China is today. Our economies were nowhere near interconnected, the way the US and the Chinese government is. So we have to get to a point where we can out compete. We're going to always be able to out innovate, and that means we've got to continue to have good schools. We have income inequality in the United States of America because we have education inequality. So we've got to make sure that our schools are producing better students. We've got to make sure that we're using tools like Pell Grant to allow workers to get you know, coding and not have to just be for a four-year institution. These are some of the conversations that we should be talking about in order to prepare for this new Cold War rather than having some of the base that we're having on issues in Washington, D.C. So, James, sorry I went on a little rant, but it's a great question and it's an important issue and I think we can get there only must.",
